Access Control (Authorization)
******************************

<div class=perex>

May the user enter the administration? Edit this article? Delete someone else's comment? These are the questions authorization answers, and in Nette each of them takes a single call to `$user->isAllowed()`:

- you write the rules in small classes, each next to the part of the application it concerns
- you decide about concrete objects, not just roles: an article may be edited by its author
- a logged-out user or a visitor never gets someone else's rights, even if you forget to think about it in your code

</div>

→ [Installation and requirements |@home#Installation]

In the examples, we will use an object of the class [api:Nette\Security\User], which represents the current user and which you get by having it passed to you using [dependency injection |dependency-injection:passing-dependencies]. In presenters, just call `$user = $this->getUser()`.

For very simple websites with administration where user permissions are not differentiated, the already known method `isLoggedIn()` can be used as the authorization criterion. In other words: as soon as a user is logged in, they have all permissions, and vice versa.

```php
if ($user->isLoggedIn()) { // is the user logged in?
	deleteItem(); // then they have permission for the operation
}
```

For such a site, this is a perfectly legitimate criterion. The voters that most of this page is about start paying off once a second kind of user, roles, or the question of who owns what come into play. If you want to ask through `isAllowed()` from the start anyway, a [single-line voter |#How Voters Reach a Decision] will do.


Roles
-----

The purpose of roles is to offer more precise control over permissions and remain independent of the username. As soon as a user logs in, they are assigned one or more roles in which they will act. Roles are simple names, for example, `admin`, `member`, `guest`, etc. They are specified as the second argument of the `SimpleIdentity` constructor, either as a string or an array of strings.

As an authorization criterion, we will now use the `isInRole()` method, which reveals whether the user is acting in the given role:

```php
if ($user->isInRole('admin')) { // is the user in the admin role?
	deleteItem(); // then they have permission for the operation
}
```

As you already know, logging out the user does not have to delete their identity. Thus, the `getIdentity()` method still returns the identity, including all granted roles. Nette Framework espouses the principle "less code, more security," where less writing leads to more secure code. Therefore, when checking roles, you do not need to verify whether the user is logged in. The `isInRole()` method works with **effective roles**: if the user is logged in, it is based on the roles specified in the identity; if not logged in, they automatically have the special role `guest` (or the roles of the [guest identity |authentication#Guest Identity], if one is provided).


May This User Edit This Article?
--------------------------------

Roles tell you who the user is, but often that is not enough. Imagine a blog where every registered user can write articles, but may edit only their own. Here the role does not decide, the particular article does: who its author is.

In our examples, an article will be a plain object carrying its ID, the ID of its author, and whether it is published and whether it is locked:

```php
class Article
{
	public function __construct(
		public int $id,
		public int $authorId,
		public bool $published = false,
		public bool $locked = false,
	) {
	}
}
```

We write the rules for articles in a separate class called a **voter**. It is a small object that decides about one kind of thing, in our case about articles. It implements the [api:Nette\Security\Voter] interface with a single method `vote()`:

```php
namespace App\Model;

use Nette\Security\{Principal, Vote, Voter};

class ArticleVoter implements Voter
{
	public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, string|\UnitEnum|null $privilege): Vote
	{
		if (!$resource instanceof Article) {
			return Vote::Abstain; // not an article, not my business
		}

		return Vote::allowIf(match ($privilege) {
			'view' => $resource->published || $principal->hasId($resource->authorId),
			'edit', 'delete' => $principal->hasId($resource->authorId),
			default => false,
		});
	}
}
```

The `vote()` method receives three things. `$principal` is the user the decision is about (why it is not `$user` itself, we will explain [in a moment |#Principal: Who the Decision Is About]). `$resource` is the **resource**, what they want to access, in our case the article. And `$privilege` is the **privilege**, the operation they want to perform on it, such as `view`, `edit` or `delete`. A resource does not have to be an object; it can also be a plain name or a class name, and we will get to both.

The answer is a vote, one of the values of the `Vote` enum. The `Vote::allowIf()` method turns a condition into either `Vote::Allow` (I allow it) or `Vote::Abstain` (I abstain, let others decide).

The `hasId()` method checks that the user is logged in and has the given ID. So a published article can be viewed by anyone, a draft only by its author, and only the author may edit or delete an article. For any other operation, the `default => false` branch makes the voter allow nothing and abstain.

How much configuration does it take for Nette to start asking the voter? You just register it as a [service |dependency-injection:services]. Nette recognizes that it implements the `Voter` interface, and you don't have to set up anything else:

```neon
services:
	- App\Model\ArticleVoter
```

If you register services automatically using the [search |dependency-injection:configuration#Search] section, you don't even need that. Nette creates a voter only when it is asked for the first time, so it may even depend on services that need `User` themselves.

Do you use the `User` class without the DI container? Then pass the voter to its constructor after the [storage |authentication#Storage for Logged-in User] and the [authenticator |authentication#Authenticator], or add it with the `addVoter()` method:

```php
$user = new Nette\Security\User($storage, $authenticator, new ArticleVoter);

// or
$user = new Nette\Security\User($storage, $authenticator);
$user->addVoter(new ArticleVoter);
```

If `$user` has no voter at all, the `isAllowed()` method throws `Nette\InvalidStateException` instead of quietly denying everything.

Other slips are not so loud. A typo in the name of a resource or privilege, or a voter you forgot to register (while you have others), simply ends in `false`. How to rule out typos in privilege names is shown in the chapter [Enums Instead of Strings |#Enums Instead of Strings]. In debug mode, the user panel in the Tracy bar lists every question asked during the request with the vote of each voter and marks those nobody decided; that is where a typo or a forgotten voter shows up.

And now the check itself. In a presenter, you ask the `$user` object, not the voter:

```php
public function actionEdit(int $id): void
{
	$article = $this->articleRepository->getById($id); // loads the article from the database
	if (!$this->getUser()->isAllowed($article, 'edit')) {
		$this->error('You cannot edit this article.', 403);
	}
	// here goes the preparation of the form for editing the article
}
```

The same goes for a template, where the `$user` variable is always available. Only those who may edit the article will see the link to edit it:

```latte
<a n:if="$user->isAllowed($article, 'edit')" n:href="edit, $article->id">Edit</a>
```

Notice that the code checking the permission has no idea who decides about it and how. The rules for articles live in one place, in `ArticleVoter`.


How Voters Reach a Decision
---------------------------

An application usually has several voters, each for its own area: one for articles, others for comments, orders or photos. Nette asks all of them every question. A voter the question does not concern abstains, and the voter in charge of that kind of thing decides.

Let's add an administrator who may do everything:

```php
class AdminVoter implements Voter
{
	public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, string|\UnitEnum|null $privilege): Vote
	{
		return Vote::allowIf($principal->isInRole('admin'));
	}
}
```

You register it as a service just like `ArticleVoter`. Without it, we would have to add the condition `isInRole('admin')` to every voter. This way `ArticleVoter` still allows only the author, `AdminVoter` allows the admin, and since one approval is enough, the admin may edit any article.

Sometimes, however, you need the opposite: a rule that nobody can override. For example, a locked article may not be edited by its author or by the admin. That is what the third vote, `Vote::Forbid`, is for. We add it to `ArticleVoter` right after the `instanceof` check:

```php
if ($resource->locked && $privilege === 'edit') {
	return Vote::Forbid; // nobody edits a locked article, not even an admin
}
```

So the result is put together by a simple rule:

- when any voter says `Forbid`, it is forbidden, no matter what the others say
- otherwise, it is enough for one voter to say `Allow`
- when all of them abstain (`Abstain`), it is forbidden

The order of voters does not matter. And forbidding is only ever deliberate: an ordinary voter returns `Vote::allowIf()`, so a plain condition can never turn into a prohibition. That is why `ArticleVoter`, which allows only the author, does not lock out the admin.

The admin may still delete a locked article, because `Forbid` applies only to the operation you wrote it for.

Be careful with `AdminVoter`, though. Nette asks every voter about every resource and every privilege, so an unconditional allow really allows everything: the sections of another kind of user, such as a customer's account, as well as operations nobody should perform, such as deleting one's own account. So narrow it down to the resources the admin is meant for, or let the voters in charge of such things say `Forbid`.

Does your site have just a single account for the administration, defined in the [configuration |configuration] under `security: users:`? Then whoever is logged in is the admin. Let the voter allow just that, but only for the cases of your [enum `Access` |#Enums Instead of Strings], so that a question about anything else never gets a silent yes:

```php
return Vote::allowIf($principal->isLoggedIn() && $resource instanceof Access && $privilege === null);
```

Why not by role? A user defined in `users:` without `roles` has an empty list of roles, the role `authenticated` known from older versions no longer exists, and a negated `isInRole('guest')` only tells you that the user lacks one particular role, not that they are logged in.

.[note]
A banned user who may not do anything at all can be handled in [authentication |authentication#Storage for Logged-in User]: when the `wakeupIdentity()` method returns `null`, Nette logs them out and to the voters they are an ordinary visitor. That, however, applies only to their own visit to the site. If the ban should also hold when [deciding for someone else |#Deciding for Someone Else], write a voter that returns `Vote::Forbid` for a banned user.


Principal: Who the Decision Is About
------------------------------------

You may be wondering why a voter does not get the `$user` object directly. There is a good reason for it, and it has to do with security.

As you already know, logging out [does not have to delete the user's identity |authentication#Identity]. That is why `$user->getId()` returns their ID even after they have logged out or their login has expired. If a voter got `$user` and compared the author of an article with its `getId()`, a logged-out user could keep editing their articles. And a visitor could edit an article that has no author at all, because both values would be `null`.

So a voter gets a `Principal`, which is the user as authorization sees them. It has an identity only when the user is really logged in, and it offers exactly what a voter needs:

- `hasId($id)` checks that this is the logged-in user with the given ID; for a visitor it always returns `false`
- `isLoggedIn()` tells whether this is a logged-in user, that is, whether the principal has an identity
- `isInRole($role)` and `getRoles()` work with the effective roles, just like on `$user`
- `getIdentity()` returns the identity of the logged-in user including their data, `null` for a visitor, even when `$user` keeps a retained identity or a [guest identity |authentication#Guest Identity]
- `getGuestIdentity()` returns the guest identity for a visitor and `null` for a logged-in user; a voter that needs the data of either one writes `$principal->getIdentity() ?? $principal->getGuestIdentity()`
- `isAllowed($resource, $privilege)` asks all the voters, which we will get to in the next chapter

The principal deliberately has no `getId()` method. You check ownership with `hasId()`, so you cannot make either of the mistakes above, even if you forget to think about them. Less code, more security.

Keep in mind that `hasId()` compares only the ID. When one `User` serves two kinds of users whose IDs come from separate tables and may overlap, such as customers and employees, combine ownership with the role: `$principal->isInRole('customer') && $principal->hasId($order->customerId)`.

The principal of the current user is returned at any time by the `$user->getPrincipal()` method.

.[note]
The `hasId()` method compares strictly, so the ID must have the same type. `SimpleIdentity` converts numeric IDs to `int`, so it matches IDs from the database; if you have your own identity with a string ID, compare with the same type.


A Voter Can Ask Another One
---------------------------

Comments have their own rules: a comment may be viewed by anyone who may view the article it belongs to, and only its author may edit it. A comment will again be a plain object:

```php
class Comment
{
	public function __construct(
		public Article $article,
		public int $authorId,
	) {
	}
}
```

We already have the rules for viewing articles in `ArticleVoter` and don't want to copy them. So `CommentVoter` asks the principal, just as you ask the `$user` object:

```php
class CommentVoter implements Voter
{
	public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, string|\UnitEnum|null $privilege): Vote
	{
		if (!$resource instanceof Comment) {
			return Vote::Abstain;
		}

		return Vote::allowIf(match ($privilege) {
			'view' => $principal->isAllowed($resource->article, 'view'),
			'edit' => $principal->hasId($resource->authorId),
			default => false,
		});
	}
}
```

The principal asks all the voters, so both `ArticleVoter` and `AdminVoter` decide whether the article is visible, exactly as if the user were asking directly. Register this voter as a service too, and you are done.


Permissions Without a Particular Object
---------------------------------------

May the user write a new article? Here we are asking about something that does not exist yet, so instead of an article we ask about its class:

```php
if ($user->isAllowed(Article::class, 'create')) {
	// shows the link for writing a new article
}
```

In `ArticleVoter`, a single condition at the beginning of the `vote()` method is enough, before the `instanceof` check. The whole voter now looks like this:

```php
class ArticleVoter implements Voter
{
	public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, string|\UnitEnum|null $privilege): Vote
	{
		if ($resource === Article::class) { // a question about articles in general
			return Vote::allowIf($privilege === 'create' && $principal->isInRole('registered'));
		}

		if (!$resource instanceof Article) {
			return Vote::Abstain; // not an article, not my business
		}

		if ($resource->locked && $privilege === 'edit') {
			return Vote::Forbid; // nobody edits a locked article, not even an admin
		}

		return Vote::allowIf(match ($privilege) {
			'view' => $resource->published || $principal->hasId($resource->authorId),
			'edit', 'delete' => $principal->hasId($resource->authorId),
			default => false,
		});
	}
}
```


Enums Instead of Strings
------------------------

Privilege names like `'edit'` are plain strings, and as you already know, a typo in them ends in a silent `false`. If you want to be sure you never mistype one, list the privileges as an enum and ask with its cases:

```php
enum ArticlePrivilege
{
	case View;
	case Edit;
	case Delete;
	case Create;
}

if ($user->isAllowed($article, ArticlePrivilege::Edit)) {
	// shows the link for editing the article
}
```

The case reaches the voter exactly as you passed it. So together with the resource, the voter checks that the privilege is a case of its enum, and then decides in a `match` by the cases:

```php
class ArticleVoter implements Voter
{
	public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, string|\UnitEnum|null $privilege): Vote
	{
		if ($resource === Article::class) { // a question about articles in general
			return Vote::allowIf($privilege === ArticlePrivilege::Create && $principal->isInRole('registered'));
		}

		if (!$resource instanceof Article || !$privilege instanceof ArticlePrivilege) {
			return Vote::Abstain; // not an article, not my business
		}

		if ($resource->locked && $privilege === ArticlePrivilege::Edit) {
			return Vote::Forbid; // nobody edits a locked article, not even an admin
		}

		return Vote::allowIf(match ($privilege) {
			ArticlePrivilege::View => $resource->published || $principal->hasId($resource->authorId),
			ArticlePrivilege::Edit, ArticlePrivilege::Delete => $principal->hasId($resource->authorId),
			ArticlePrivilege::Create => false, // a new article is asked about by its class
		});
	}
}
```

After the `instanceof` check, `$privilege` can only be one of the cases of `ArticlePrivilege`, so the `match` needs no `default` branch. And because it lists every case, static analysis such as PHPStan warns you when you add a new privilege to the enum and forget about it in the voter.

What do you gain? A typo in the name of a case, such as `ArticlePrivilege::Edti`, is an error PHP reports the moment the code reaches it, and static analysis even earlier. It can never turn into a silent denial. Nor can a mix-up get through: for a case of another enum, such as `CommentPrivilege::Edit`, the voter abstains. The price is a longer notation in templates, where you write the enum by its full name including the namespace: `$user->isAllowed($article, App\Model\ArticlePrivilege::Edit)`.

For a voter, the name `'edit'` and the case `ArticlePrivilege::Edit` are two different things: asked with the name, this voter abstains. So pick one way for each kind of thing and stick to it everywhere: in presenters, in templates and in voters that ask other voters, like `CommentVoter`. The examples on the rest of this page stay with names.

The enum needs no values. Give it string values only when the rules of the [ACL |#Rules for Roles and Sections of the Site] should apply to the privileges too: the ACL works with names, and from a case of an enum backed by strings it takes the value, so a case declared as `case Edit = 'edit'` follows its rules for `edit`.

An enum can serve as the resource too. When a permission concerns no object, for example access to the reports or to the administration, you pass a case of an enum as the resource and leave out the privilege:

```php
enum Access
{
	case Administration;
	case Reports;
}

$user->isAllowed(Access::Reports);

// in the voter
return Vote::allowIf($resource === Access::Reports && $privilege === null && $principal->isInRole('editor'));
```

Notice the condition `$privilege === null`. Without it, the voter would allow any question about the reports, even `$user->isAllowed(Access::Reports, ArticlePrivilege::Delete)`, whose meaning nobody has ever defined.

Let a single voter decide the whole enum `Access`. It checks the resource and the privilege once at the start and then lists every case in a `match`, so static analysis warns you when you add a new case and forget about it:

```php
if (!$resource instanceof Access || $privilege !== null) {
	return Vote::Abstain;
}

return Vote::allowIf(match ($resource) {
	Access::Administration => $principal->isInRole('admin'),
	Access::Reports => $principal->isInRole('editor'),
});
```

Another voter that needs such a permission does not repeat its rule, it asks `$principal->isAllowed(Access::Reports)`. A presenter or an action reserved for it says so by the attribute `#[Requires(allowed: Access::Administration)]`, see [How to Use the Requires Attribute |best-practices:attribute-requires#User Permission].

So when to choose which? A case of an enum as the resource suits a permission that stands on its own, without any object. When there is a thing behind the permission, ask about the object and say what you want to do with it by a privilege from an enum. Creating a new thing is asked about its parent, for example a new topic by `$user->isAllowed($board, BoardPrivilege::Post)`; where there is no parent, it is a permission of its own, a case of `Access`. A class name like `Article::class` works as a resource too, but then one enum serves two kinds of resources and the voter has to sort out which privilege belongs to which. With a link, ask about what its target enforces, so that it never offers a page that then turns the user away.

Several voters can also share one enum, for example a common `Privilege` with the cases `View`, `Edit` and `Delete` for both articles and comments. Each voter then answers only for the cases that make sense for its resource. List the others in the `match` explicitly with `false`, such as `Privilege::Delete => false`, instead of a `default` branch: when you add a new case to the enum, static analysis shows you every voter that has to decide about it.


Deciding for Someone Else
-------------------------

Sometimes you need to ask about the permissions of a user other than the one currently browsing the site. For example, when sending notifications about a new article that should go only to those who may read it. That is what the `createPrincipal()` method is for, which you call in any service that has `Nette\Security\User` passed to it:

```php
foreach ($this->subscriberRepository->getIdentities() as $identity) { // identities of all subscribers
	if ($this->user->createPrincipal($identity)->isAllowed($article, 'view')) {
		// sends the subscriber an e-mail about the new article
	}
}
```

The principal asks the same voters as `$user`, but it does not affect the login of the current user in any way. It takes the identity exactly as you pass it: it does not call the authenticator's `wakeupIdentity()` method for it and stores nothing. So load it from the database in its current form, with the current roles. And because the principal may be someone else than the visitor of the current request, a voter must not read anything that belongs to that request and its user, such as the session; what is the same for everyone, such as the configuration of the site, is fine.

Instead of an identity, you can pass `null` and ask what an anonymous visitor may do. This comes in handy, for example, when generating an RSS feed or a sitemap, which should contain only the articles everyone can see:

```php
$visitor = $this->user->createPrincipal(null);
if ($visitor->isAllowed($article, 'view')) {
	// adds the article to the RSS feed
}
```

A voter decides about one object at a time, and these examples ask about a single article. When you go through large sets, such as listings, feeds, sitemaps or notifications to thousands of users, asking about every row is slow. Filter right in the database query instead, for example by selecting only published articles, and make sure the condition in the query agrees with what the voter says.


Testing Voters
--------------

A voter is a plain class that needs neither a session nor a login, which makes it easy to test. You create the principal yourself: the first argument is the identity of the logged-in user (or `null` for a visitor), the second their roles (leave them out and those of the identity are used). The example uses [Nette Tester |tester:]:

```php
use Nette\Security\{Principal, SimpleIdentity, Vote};
use Tester\Assert;

$voter = new ArticleVoter;
$draft = new Article(id: 1, authorId: 1);

$author = new Principal(new SimpleIdentity(1), ['registered']);
Assert::same(Vote::Allow, $voter->vote($author, $draft, 'edit'));

$guest = new Principal(null, ['guest']);
Assert::same(Vote::Abstain, $voter->vote($guest, $draft, 'edit'));
```

If you want to test how voters work together, pass them to the principal as the third argument and ask its `isAllowed()` method:

```php
$guest = new Principal(null, voters: [new ArticleVoter, new CommentVoter]);
Assert::false($guest->isAllowed(new Comment($draft, authorId: 2), 'view'));
```

To check that the voters are registered in the container and work together as in the application, ask a principal created by `$user->createPrincipal()` of the user from the container. A test of a presenter with a logged-in user gets by with the login kept in memory, which you switch on in the test configuration by `security: authentication: storage: memory`.


Rules for Roles and Sections of the Site
----------------------------------------

Not every permission concerns an object. For example, only editors may enter the administration, whichever of its pages it is. Rules like these between roles and sections of the site are conveniently written in the [ACL |acl] that comes with Nette. Just list them in the [configuration]:

```neon
security:
	roles:
		guest:
		registered: [guest]
		editor: [registered]
		admin: [registered]

	resources:
		backend:

	rules:
		allow:
			- [editor, backend]
```

To Nette, the ACL is just another voter, so you ask in exactly the same way. This time the resource is the name of the section, and we leave out the privilege, because we are interested in the section as a whole:

```php
if (!$this->getUser()->isAllowed('backend')) {
	$this->error('Forbidden', 403);
}
```

Leaving out the privilege means passing `null`. To your own voter, that simply says no particular privilege was asked; it is the ACL that reads `null` as all privileges (and a left-out resource as all resources).

Voters and the ACL complement each other: the ACL decides by roles and section names, voters about particular objects. You can read how the ACL works and everything it can do in the chapter [ACL |acl].

.[note]
Your own authorizator implementing `Nette\Security\Authorizator` that decides by roles still works, but it is deprecated, so rewrite it as a voter, as shown in [upgrading to version 4.0 |upgrading#Authorization].

{{maintitle: PHP Authorization with Nette: Roles and Voters}}
